  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/fbbddeb527a9ea78b274fd7c4afe9809546a61ba833a67a385cb18aac9e14942.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $1$ unit to the right to give $y={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(1,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/617dbba0d17c30ce03da929550d17062fd644ed7992a07b55caa02e5815e7232.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}.$ The point $(1,1)$ moves to $(1,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/7e4d38e401c4ce01330d6082dadd43e50974a55b666023d8b8d51bbcbc7f7258.png)",
